<Item XName="Page" Type="PageMgr" />
<Item XName="READ_ME" UserName="READ_ME" Type="Notes">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Phased Array with S-Parameters&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies SystemVue Example&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;The objective of this&amp;nbsp;example is to&amp;nbsp;demonstrate the use of the ArraySParam model. The ArraySParam is used to model a generic N-input to M-output (total N+M ports) device characterized using S-parameters in a phased array design. A Rx Combiner and a Tx Phase Shifter test cases are included.&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Rx Folder&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;In the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1Combiner&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; design in this folder, the ArraySParam model is used to model a 16-to-1 combiner with SParameter data. For comparison purposes, the design &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1CombinerRef &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;implements the same system but using the &quot;&lt;I style=&quot;mso-bidi-font-style: normal&quot;&gt;ideal&lt;/I&gt;&quot; ArraySplit model to implement the 16-to-1 combiner.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;The graph &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Splitter_SParams &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;shows the S-Parameters for the 17-port, 16-to-1 combiner. Open the graph and tune the PortIdx variable with the slider provided on the graph to see S(N,PortIdx) responses, where N=1, 2, ..., 17.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Run the analyses &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1CombinerAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1CombinerRefAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and then run the equation &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;PlotRxPatterns&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;. Observe the differences between &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Rx Beam&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; (from the design using SParameters) and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Rx Beam Ref&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; (from the design using the &quot;ideal&quot; ArraySplit). You can also see the difference in the receiver output power in the table &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;RxOut&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;In the &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Tune&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; window &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Analyses To Run&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; area, select &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1CombinerAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1CombinerRefAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and then tune the variable &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Finc_GHz&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;. Observe the differences in the beam patterns and the receiver output power levels.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Tx Folder&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;In the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifter &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;design in this folder, the ArraySParam model is used to model an RFIC implementing 16 parallel phase shifters that can be controlled to steer the beam to 5 different preconfigured directions. For comparison purposes, the design &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterRef&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; implements the same system but using the &quot;ideal&quot; ArrayPhase model.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;The equation &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;TxConfigure&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; defines 5 sets of&amp;nbsp;16 phase shift values that will steer the beam at 5 different directions (see &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;thetas&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;phis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; variables in same equation). These 5 sets have been calculated for the frequency of 10 GHz. The tunable variable &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;pick&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; is used to select one of these sets. The selected set of phase shift values is stored in the variable &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;PhaseShifts&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;, which is used in the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterRef &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;design.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;In the design &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifter&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;, the ArraySParam model is set to have 5 states (&lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;NumStates&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; parameter) and the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;State&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; parameter is set to the tunable variable &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;pick&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;, to select one of the 5 states of operation. Each state of operation is modeled by a different 32-port SParameter file. You &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;ONLY&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; have to specify &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;one&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; SParameter file in the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;FileName&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; parameter of the ArraySParam component, &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;as long as&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; you follow a convention for naming the set of SParameter files that will be used. The convention is:&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;lt;BaseName&amp;gt;&amp;lt;StateNumber&amp;gt;.&amp;lt;OptionalArbitraryDescriptiveText&amp;gt;.sNp. &lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Using SParameters to model the operation of this set of 16 phase shifters allows us to include coupling effects between the 16 outputs (the specific SParameter files used in this example do not include any coupling effects) as well as model frequency dependent behavior. The 5 SParameter files used in this example have data from 9 GHz to 20 GHz with 1 GHz step and at the frequency of 10 GHz the phase shifts introduced are exactly the same as the ones used in the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterRef &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;design.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Run the analyses &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterAnalysis &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterRefAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and then run the equation &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;PlotTxPatterns&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;. Observe the differences between T&lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;x Beam&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; (from the design using SParameters) and T&lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;x Beam Ref&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; (from the design using the &quot;ideal&quot; ArrayPhase).&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;In &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Tune&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; window &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Analyses To Run&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; area, select &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterAnalysis &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifterRefAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;(unselect &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Param16to1CombinerAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SParam16to1CombinerRefAnalysis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;),&amp;nbsp;make sure &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Fc_GHz&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; is set to 10, and then tune the variable &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;pick&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;. You can use the slider provided on either Tx schematic since they are identical sliders. Also you can use the tune window to do this but make sure you stay with the pick range (1&#x2264; pick &#x2264; 5). &lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Notice that the beam patterns are identical. You can place maximum markers to track the beam peak. It should match the corresponding value in the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;thetas&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; and &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;phis&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; variables in&amp;nbsp;the equation &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;TxConfigure&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;Now fix the value of the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;pick&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; variable and tune &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;Fc_GHz&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt;. In addition to squinting effects (due to the fact that the phase shifts for the reference design were computed for 10 GHz), you can see the additional effects due to the frequency dependent behavior in the beam pattern of the &lt;EM&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&apos;FONT-FAMILY: &quot;Arial&quot;,sans-serif&apos;&gt;SparamPhaseShifter&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/EM&gt; design.&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;</Text>
